    'excerpt'     => 'If your cat only drools when they\'re curled up purring on your lap, you can probably relax. If the drooling shows up with bad breath, dropped food, or a cat who suddenly won\'t eat, that\'s a different conversation, and this is how to tell which one you\'re dealing with.',
    'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>Start with the question most people actually have: is a wet patch on your sleeve or pillow something to worry about? For a lot of cats, no. Plenty of cats drool a little when they're deeply relaxed, purring, kneading a blanket, or half-asleep on your chest, and that's considered a normal, if slightly odd, feline habit. It's the drooling that shows up <em>without</em> the contentment, or alongside other symptoms, that deserves a closer look.</p>

<p>The honest answer is that nobody fully knows why relaxed drooling happens. A Cornell behavior specialist has said there's no study that's actually pinned down the physiology behind drooling while purring, though one common theory ties it to kneading and nursing behavior carried over from kittenhood, since many "happy droolers" have done it their whole lives. What vets do agree on is this: dental disease is the single most common medical reason cats drool, and it's usually the first thing to rule out when drooling is new or excessive.</p>

<h2>The Drooling That's Probably Fine</h2>

<p>A few patterns tend to be harmless. If your cat only drools while purring, kneading, being petted in a favorite spot, or drifting off to sleep, and they're eating normally, acting normally, and not losing weight, that's very likely just how this particular cat expresses contentment. Some cats have done this since kittenhood and never grow out of it. Breed doesn't change the basic rules here, though a few flat-faced breeds and individual cats are simply more prone to a bit of drool than others.</p>

<p>Stress-drooling fits in this category too, even though it doesn't feel pleasant to watch. A cat who drools in the carrier, at the vet's office, or during a car ride, usually alongside flattened ears, loud vocalizing, or trying to hide, is reacting to fear rather than illness. It tends to stop as soon as the stressful situation ends.</p>

<p>The common thread in "normal" drooling is that it comes and goes with an obvious trigger, and nothing else about the cat changes. Appetite stays the same, breath doesn't suddenly turn foul, and there's no new pawing at the face or hiding.</p>

<h2>Where It Stops Being Just a Quirk</h2>

<h3>Dental disease, by far the most common culprit</h3>

<p>If a cat's drooling is new, constant, or getting heavier over weeks, start by assuming it's the mouth. Tartar buildup leads to gingivitis, which leads to sore gums and, in cats specifically, a painful condition called tooth resorption, where the body starts breaking down a tooth from the inside out. None of that is comfortable, and a sore mouth produces more saliva, often tinged pink from irritated gums, sometimes smelling noticeably worse than usual. Cats are also good at hiding dental pain. They don't always stop eating; they just chew differently, favor one side, or drop food more than usual. Our <a href="/tools/cat-dental-care-guide/">cat dental care guide</a> covers what dental disease looks like day to day and how often it needs professional attention.</p>

<h3>Nausea</h3>

<p>Drooling that shows up right before vomiting, or that comes with a reduced appetite and a cat who seems "off" rather than in pain, points toward the stomach rather than the mouth. Hairballs, a sudden diet change, eating too fast, or an underlying digestive issue can all trigger this kind of ptyalism. If a cat is drooling and also skipping meals, it's worth reading through our <a href="/tools/cat-not-eating-guide/">cat not eating guide</a>, since the two symptoms together usually mean the same thing is bothering them.</p>

<h3>Something stuck in the mouth</h3>

<p>Cats get string, bone fragments, plant material, and the occasional blade of grass wedged against the roof of the mouth or caught around the base of the tongue. This usually causes sudden, heavy drooling, along with gagging, pawing at the face, or a cat who keeps opening and closing their mouth strangely. It's uncomfortable enough that most cats will show you something is wrong even if you can't see what. A quick look inside the mouth with a flashlight is reasonable if your cat will tolerate it; forcing it open against real resistance is not.</p>

<h3>Oral ulcers</h3>

<p>Sores inside the mouth or on the tongue can come from a caustic substance a cat licked, from a viral infection, or from other underlying illness. They're painful in the same way a mouth ulcer is painful for a person, just harder to see, and they tend to produce the same combination of drooling, reluctance to eat, and bad breath that dental disease does.</p>

<h3>Heatstroke</h3>

<p>Cats are less prone to overheating than dogs, partly because they're less likely to exercise themselves into trouble, but it still happens, especially to cats left in hot cars, enclosed rooms, or carriers in direct sun, and to flat-faced breeds that can't pant efficiently. Drooling alongside heavy breathing, panting, weakness, or stumbling after heat exposure is an emergency, not a wait-and-see situation.</p>

<h3>Toxins and plants</h3>

<p>Certain household plants and chemicals irritate the mouth and esophagus or trigger drooling through the nervous system. Lilies get most of the attention for cats because of kidney toxicity, but plenty of ordinary houseplants and cleaning products can cause a mouthful of drool within minutes of contact. If you also keep seasonal plants around, our post on <a href="/blog/is-christmas-cactus-poisonous-to-cats/">whether Christmas cactus is poisonous to cats</a> covers one specific case; the broader point is that sudden drooling right after your cat was near a plant or chemical is worth treating as a possible exposure. If you suspect your cat actually ate or licked something toxic, go straight to our <a href="/tools/pet-poison-emergency-guide/">pet poison emergency guide</a> rather than waiting to see what happens.</p>

<h2>A Side-by-Side Look</h2>

<p>It's rarely one clean symptom in isolation. What usually tips the scale from "probably fine" to "call the vet" is what's happening around the drooling.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Signs it's likely normal</th><th>Signs it needs a vet</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Only happens while purring, kneading, or sleeping</td><td>Happens randomly, or is new and getting worse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appetite and energy are unchanged</td><td>Eating less, chewing oddly, or dropping food</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stops once a stressful event (carrier, vet) ends</td><td>Comes with pawing at the mouth or face</td></tr>
<tr><td>Breath smells normal</td><td>Breath suddenly smells bad, or saliva looks pink or thick</td></tr>
<tr><td>No vomiting, hiding, or lethargy</td><td>Paired with vomiting, hiding, panting, or weakness</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What To Actually Do About It</h2>

<p>For a cat who only drools in happy, relaxed moments and is otherwise completely normal, there's nothing to do beyond keeping a towel handy. For everything else, a few things help a vet get to the answer faster: note when the drooling started, whether it's constant or comes and goes, whether your cat has been near any plants, chemicals, or medications recently, and whether eating, grooming, or litter box habits have changed. A quick look at the gums and teeth at home, done gently and without forcing the mouth open, can sometimes reveal an obvious culprit like a broken tooth or visible sore, but a full exam under better lighting is usually needed to find anything below the surface.</p>

<p>Suspected toxin exposure or heatstroke symptoms (heavy panting, weakness, collapse) should go to a vet or emergency clinic immediately, not wait for a next-day appointment. Everything else, including dental pain and most cases of nausea, is worth a scheduled visit rather than an emergency one, unless your cat has also stopped eating or drinking entirely.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Why does my cat drool when I pet them?</h3>
<p>Most likely contentment. Some cats drool when they're deeply relaxed or purring, a trait that seems to start in kittenhood for many of them. As long as eating, energy, and breath are all normal, this isn't a health concern.</p>

<h3>Is it normal for cats to drool in their sleep?</h3>
<p>It can be. A cat sleeping deeply enough to fully relax their jaw muscles may let a little saliva escape, similar to the purring-and-kneading pattern. It becomes worth checking out if it's heavy, constant, or new.</p>

<h3>Why is my cat drooling thick saliva?</h3>
<p>Thick, ropey, or discolored saliva (pink, yellow-tinged, or foul-smelling) is more consistent with a mouth problem, such as gum disease or an ulcer, than with relaxed drooling. It's a reasonable reason to book a dental check.</p>

<h3>Why is my cat drooling and not eating?</h3>
<p>This combination usually points to pain somewhere in the mouth, or to nausea. Either way, a cat that's drooling and skipping meals for more than a day needs a vet visit rather than a wait-and-see approach.</p>

<h3>Can heatstroke really cause drooling in cats?</h3>
<p>Yes, though it's less common than in dogs. A cat drooling alongside heavy breathing, panting, or weakness after time in a hot space (a parked car, an unventilated carrier, direct sun) should be cooled down and seen urgently.</p>

<h3>What does it mean if a cat suddenly starts drooling a lot?</h3>
<p>Sudden, heavy drooling with no obvious happy trigger is one of the more reliable signals that something physical is wrong, whether that's an object stuck in the mouth, a toxin, or acute nausea. It's worth same-day attention rather than monitoring it for a week.</p>

<h3>Do some cats just drool more than others?</h3>
<p>Yes. Individual cats, and a handful of flat-faced breeds, are simply more prone to drooling in general, especially when relaxed. That's a personality trait rather than a symptom, as long as nothing else is off.</p>

